# The Sparrow with Four Sexes

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 23](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_023.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *White Stripe*

@body

![Slide image 24](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_024.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Tan Stripe*

@body

![Slide image 25](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_025.png)

=== layout: two-panels


@left

![Slide image 25](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_024.png)

@right

![Slide image 25](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_025.png)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 26](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_026.png)

- Remarkably, [white birds]{color=gray} of either sex almost exclusively mate with [tan birds]{color=#E8A202} of the opposite sex.
- A pattern which results in four effective &#8216;sexes&#8217;, as any given individual can breed with only one-quarter (25%) of the population

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 27](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/image_027.png)

- [White]{color=gray} and [tan]{color=#E8A202} individuals also show alternative reproductive strategies\:
  - [White males]{color=gray} are aggressive, provide little parental care, and are promiscuous, whereas [tan males]{color=#E8A202} are monogamous and invest highly in rearing their nestlings
  - [White females]{color=gray} are more aggressive and solicit more copulations from their mates than do [tan females]{color=#E8A202}

=== layout: one-panel

# *How Four Sexes*

@body

![Slide image 28](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/sparrow-supergene.drawn.svg)

- An ancient mutation inverted a large section of chromosome 2, creating a supergene.
- Reduced effective genetic exchange lets the two versions diverge and accumulate mutations.
- Their divergence is thought to resemble the evolution of mammalian XY and bird ZW sex chromosomes.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 29](assets/lect13b-chromo_alter_3-inversion/sparrow-offspring.drawn.svg)

- Males mate with females; white-stripes almost always mate with tan-stripes (disassortative mating).
- On average, half the offspring inherit the inverted chromosome 2: half tan-striped and half white-striped.

=== layout: one-panel

